@Test
void throwsWithClearMessageOnZeroDivisor() {
    var ex = assertThrows(
        IllegalArgumentException.class,
        () -> calculator.divide(10, 0)); // exactly this call must throw

    assertEquals("divisor must not be zero", ex.getMessage());
}

@Test
void doesNotThrowForValidInput() {
    assertDoesNotThrow(() -> calculator.divide(10, 2)); // negative case
}
```
Because `assertThrows` returns the exception, you can assert the message, the cause, or downcast to a custom exception and check its fields.

